^^Countries and nationalities^^

In this skill you will learn the names of different countries, their inhabitants and the names of languages in Finnish.

Importantly, you will be introduced to your first noun suffixes! Suffixes are endings that you put onto a noun and that change or add meaning to the base word, much like when conjugating a verb. The endings you will learn now is the *-sta/-stä*-ending (denoting *from* something) and the *-aan/-een/-iin*-endings (denoting *to* something). For example:

Suomi - (Finland)
Suome*sta* - (from Finland)
Suom*een* - (to Finland)

Saksa - (Germany)
Saksa*sta* - (from Germany)
Saks*aan* - (to Germany)

Venäjä - (Russia)
Venäjä*ltä* - (from Russia)
Venäjä*lle* - (to Russia)

At first, it might seem confusing that all of these countries seem to get different endings than the others, even though it is the country that changes, not the meaning. This is partly because the endings change their vowels depending on which vowel the preceding word ends in (vowel harmony), and partly because different countries require different specifics. 

For example, in English you would say '*on* Iceland' and in German 'in *der* Ukraine', although these are both countries that logically speaking shouldn't require extra words. 

Finnish takes this one step further, for example if you want to say 'in Russia' it is not *Venäjässä* (inside Russia) but *Venäjällä* (on Russia). Similarly, Kypros (Cyprus) becomes *Kyproksella* (on Cyprus), the ending '-lla' denoting 'on', and *Ahvenanmaa* (Åland island) becomes *Ahvenanmaa*lla* (on the Åland island), but Alankomaat (the Netherlands) becomes *Alankomaissa* (lit. inside the Netherlands). Lastly, Suomi (Finland) is an exception because it is changed in the exact same way as Ruotsi (Sweden) except 'i' is changed to an 'e' (*Suomessa* vs *Ruotsissa*)

There are many endings, and it is better to learn the different countries by heart because it will be easier to grasp the endings when we dive into them later. If you want a sneak peek though, here is a list of the endings we will go through in the future:


English             Finnish

inside          	--> -ssa/-ssä
from inside     --> -sta/-stä
into            	--> -een/-aan/-*n (endings correspond with vowel harmony)

on              	--> -lla/-llä
from on top of --> -lta/-ltä
onto            	 --> -lle


Example: *kirja* (book)

kirjassa    (in the book)
kirjasta    (from the book, about the book)
kirjaan     (into the book)

kirjalla    (on the book)
kirjalta    (from on top of the book)
kirjalle    (onto the book)